Q: Java self referencing generic type A: <p>There are two issues with Self type that I can think of : contra variance of parameters, and constructors</p>
<h1>Contra-variance of parameters</h1>
<p>If a <em>Vehicle</em> can embark <em>People</em> , then a <em>Car</em> which is a <strong>subtype</strong> of <em>Vehicle</em> can also embark <em>People</em>. It can't just embark <em>Japanese</em> people, or just <em>Children</em>. It can however embark <em>Mobile</em> objects which is a <strong>supertype</strong> of <em>People</em>.</p>
<p>That is contra variance of parameters : in a <strong>sub</strong> type, the parameters must be a <strong>super</strong> type (can be the same)</p>
<p>Therefore, a Self type would be invalid as a parameter, since it would be a <strong>sub</strong> type in the child class. This means, Self can't be used for parameters</p>
<p>This is not a problem for return types, which are <em>covariant</em> instead : in a sub type, the return type must be a sub type (can be the same).<br />
If a <em>Vehicle</em> gives a <em>Receipt</em> upon embarking, then a <em>Car</em> can give a <em>PaperReceipt</em> , but not a supertype <em>Document</em> .</p>
<h1>Constructing Self</h1>
<p>Let's say a <em>Vehicle</em> can be <code>clone()</code> ton return <em>Self</em> . How do you construct that Self ? Well you can't because any method that return a <code>new X()</code> will be invalid : let's say your <em>Car</em>'s <code>clone()</code> return a <code>new Car</code>, then your <em>ElectricCar</em> will also return a <em>Car</em> which is not <em>Self</em>.</p>
<p>You thus need to override each method returning Self, which defeats the point of OOP.</p>
<p>The only <em>Self</em> that you reliably have access to, is <code>this</code>. Which means that <em>Self</em> returning methods should be <code>void</code> method that the compiler actually make return this at the end.</p>
<h1>Valid usage</h1>
<p>This is still pretty useful, for example</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>class NumberStore {
  Self writeOnly();
  Self withCacheExpire(int seconds);
}

class IntStore extends NumberStore{
  IntStore store(int value);
}

class Test {
  void foo(){
    myIntStore.writeOnly().withCacheExpire(12).store(12);
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>But is this small usage worth changing the language ? Well, I do think it is, but I'm not sure there is no other caveat.</p>

Q: org.h2.jdbc.JdbcSQLException: Connection is broken: &quot;java.net.ConnectException: Connection refused: connect: localhost&quot; [90067-193] A: <p>i figured out that , for H2 to work with tcp , you need to start H2 in server mode</p>
<p>to start the server in server mode , you need to create a TCP server , so below configuration will give you connetion for 1 application.</p>
<pre><code>import org.h2.tools.Server;

     @Bean(initMethod = &quot;start&quot;, destroyMethod = &quot;stop&quot;)
        public Server h2Server1() throws SQLException {
            return Server.createTcpServer(&quot;-tcp&quot;, &quot;-tcpAllowOthers&quot;, &quot;-tcpPort&quot;, &quot;9080&quot;);
        }
</code></pre>
<p>if you want more applications to connect to same H2 server with TCP , you need to add one more instance to it - with different port</p>
<pre><code> // Second App
    @Bean(initMethod = &quot;start&quot;, destroyMethod = &quot;stop&quot;)
    public Server h2Server2() throws SQLException {
        return Server.createTcpServer(&quot;-tcp&quot;, &quot;-tcpAllowOthers&quot;, &quot;-tcpPort&quot;, &quot;9081&quot;);
    }
</code></pre>

Q: Access service into static method in Angular 5 A: <p>First of all let's address that <code>backend</code> property is not static, so it can't really be accessed within the static method <code>myMethod</code>.</p>
<p>I'd like to talk about answer to the question first. Then I will try to address flaws in the design and possible solution to these flaws.</p>
<p>In order to access injected service from a static method, you need an injector instance. You can create injector instance in-place (right before <code>myComponent</code> declaration) like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>let injector = Injector.create({providers: [
    { provide: BackendService, deps: [] }
  ]});
</code></pre>
<p>Or declare a simple helper file <code>app-injector.ts</code></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>export let appInjector: Injector;


export function setAppInjector(injector: Injector) {
    appInjector = injector;
}
</code></pre>
<p>And apply injector from AppModule:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>export class AppModule {
    constructor(injector: Injector) {
        setAppInjector(injector);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then in your component you can import <code>app-injector.ts</code> file and in the static method you can just do</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>
public static myMethod() {
  appInjector.get(BackendService).getData()
    .subscribe(
      data =&gt; { console.log(data)   },
      error =&gt; { console.error(error); }
  );
</code></pre>
<p>There is serious flaw in this design actually.
Why use static method in your component in the first place?
By making method static you emphasize that it doesn't belong to the component. It actually belongs to some business logic which must be separated from the component. Why not placing business logic into the service?</p>
<ul>
<li>It would make coupling between a visual component and a business logic more loose</li>
<li>It would improved overall system testability</li>
<li>Such code would be following angular coding style guides more strictly.</li>
</ul>

Q: Alternative to set duplicateParentState to true for each child view? A: <p>How about extending the widget -like you said- and overriding <a href="http://developer.android.com/reference/android/view/ViewGroup.html#onLayout(boolean,%20int,%20int,%20int,%20int)" rel="nofollow">onLayout()</a>? This way you ensure to modify the children state also the first time the widget is drawn. When onLayout() is called, getChildCount() will always return the actual number of children. For example:</p>

<pre><code>public class LinearLayout extends android.widget.LinearLayout {

  private static final int[] VIEW_GROUP_ATTRS = new int[]{ android.R.attr.enabled };
  private static final boolean DEFAULT_IS_ENABLED = true;

  private boolean isEnabled = DEFAULT_IS_ENABLED;

  public LinearLayout(Context context) {
    super(context);
  }

  public LinearLayout(Context context, AttributeSet attrs) {
    super(context, attrs);
    isEnabled = context.obtainStyledAttributes(attrs, VIEW_GROUP_ATTRS).getBoolean(0, DEFAULT_IS_ENABLED);
  }


  @Override
  public void setEnabled(boolean enabled) {
    super.setEnabled(enabled);
    this.isEnabled = enabled;

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; getChildCount(); ++i) {
      getChildAt(i).setEnabled(enabled);
    }
  }

  @Override
  protected void onLayout(boolean changed, int left, int top, int right, int bottom) {
    super.onLayout(changed, left, top, right, bottom);
    setEnabled(isEnabled);
  }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Note that you are actually extending the widget (calling super on any method being overridden).</p>
